160
00:10:08,840 --> 00:10:12,000
that I've talked about before and I've told stories about before.

161
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:16,320
There are a couple of things that I think are much more important

162
00:10:16,320 --> 00:10:18,240
for us to derive from this.

163
00:10:18,240 --> 00:10:21,120
Scouting has a century long history

164
00:10:21,560 --> 00:10:24,360
of having people who have been

165
00:10:24,800 --> 00:10:28,600
very key leaders in the scouting movement

166
00:10:29,200 --> 00:10:32,960
and very key leaders in scouting organizations,

167
00:10:32,960 --> 00:10:36,720
And there's a big difference between organizations and movements.

168
00:10:37,160 --> 00:10:40,400
The scouting movement is is this thing

169
00:10:40,400 --> 00:10:45,160
that kind of grew out of Baden Powell's ideas,

170
00:10:45,720 --> 00:10:48,080
without a whole lot of organization.

171
00:10:48,600 --> 00:10:50,760
It just began happening

172
00:10:50,760 --> 00:10:54,240
And organizations came later and organizations do their best

173
00:10:54,560 --> 00:10:59,640
to contain whatever it is that the scouting movement is. OK.

174
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:05,000
So I want to begin talking about something that I, you know.

175
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:06,960
I'm not too sure exactly what to call it.

176
00:11:07,480 --> 00:11:12,480
The best thing that I've come up with is the source code for scouting.

177
00:11:12,600 --> 00:11:17,280
There are big software programs out there that are open source

178
00:11:17,720 --> 00:11:22,079
and they have a source code and you can use the source code for free

179
00:11:22,439 --> 00:11:23,880
to create an application.

180
00:11:23,880 --> 00:11:26,600
And I think it's a pretty good allegory for what I'm talking about,

181
00:11:26,600 --> 00:11:31,040
because whatever the scouting movement is, you can take that source code

182
00:11:31,040 --> 00:11:34,600
and you can apply it in your community and you end up having scouting.

183
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:37,800
And it doesn't depend on an organization,

184
00:11:38,120 --> 00:11:41,959
as long as it contains the source code, it's scouting.

185
00:11:42,319 --> 00:11:46,160
And we're in this period of our history as an organization

186
00:11:46,680 --> 00:11:50,520
in the United States, and we're in this period of history

187
00:11:50,600 --> 00:11:55,640
where things are changing and growing and and our organizational structures

188
00:11:55,640 --> 00:11:59,439
may be able to respond to those changes and they may not.

189
00:11:59,680 --> 00:12:05,240
But the source code of scouting is what's going to work all of the time.

190
00:12:05,439 --> 00:12:08,520
OK, It's what's worked for the past century

191
00:12:08,520 --> 00:12:11,040
and it's what will continue to work.

192
00:12:11,240 --> 00:12:15,199
Here's the beginning of this story of one man's discovery

193
00:12:15,600 --> 00:12:19,480
of scouting. So let's, let's listen to it up to that point.

194
00:12:20,560 --> 00:12:23,560
Well, I'll just tell you about how I grew up and what happened to me,

195
00:12:23,560 --> 00:12:27,560
because I was born in Denmark on the 6th of August 1900..

196
00:12:27,880 --> 00:12:34,240
When I was 10, 1910, I got a special gift for Christmas

197
00:12:35,120 --> 00:12:39,800
and that was a Danish translational big powers book- Scouting for Boys,

198
00:12:40,080 --> 00:12:42,680
And I got very excited about it because there'd been a lot of

199
00:12:42,680 --> 00:12:44,920
cyber oil after that particular time.

200
00:12:45,920 --> 00:12:48,560
Then I started reading about all of the things that could be

201
00:12:48,560 --> 00:12:50,440
doing if I became a boy scout.

202
00:12:50,440 --> 00:12:54,400
I could go hiking and camping and exploring and hiking

203
00:12:54,400 --> 00:12:56,840
and finding wild animals and so on.

204
00:12:57,720 --> 00:13:02,440
I spent the whole Christmas reading about scouting

205
00:13:03,240 --> 00:13:05,760
And then in those days it was very easy to become a scout.

206
00:13:06,400 --> 00:13:09,560
The Danish Boy Scouts Association hadn't been established,

207
00:13:10,520 --> 00:13:12,840
So we just sat in a boat and if you want to become a scout,

208
00:13:12,840 --> 00:13:17,080
all you do is you get together with a few of your friends,

209
00:13:17,080 --> 00:13:19,480
you'll pick up a patrol leader, you'll go out and do scouting.

210
00:13:19,560 --> 00:13:22,760
So you just start your the whole thing all by yourself,

211
00:13:22,760 --> 00:13:25,440
because it was nothing but really being organized.

212
00:13:25,440 --> 00:13:28,520
So that's exactly what I did during my Christmas vacation.

213
00:13:28,600 --> 00:13:31,160
I'd hold with some of my friends and we formed a patrol

214
00:13:31,160 --> 00:13:33,640
and we called it the Tiger Patrol-

215
00:13:34,200 --> 00:13:36,560
We had to have a fire fancy name for that-

216
00:13:37,520 --> 00:13:40,560
And then we got along all right in the beginning,

217
00:13:40,560 --> 00:13:43,600
but then there were always things that might happen

218
00:13:43,600 --> 00:13:46,320
and jealousy and things like that.

219
00:13:46,320 --> 00:13:48,640
We hadn't quite picked up the scout spirit yet,

220
00:13:49,600 --> 00:13:55,200
But we stuck together and said all about the summer vacation,

221
00:13:55,200 --> 00:13:57,360
and then you just split up and then you had to start

222
00:13:57,360 --> 00:13:58,400
all over again,

223
00:13:59,120 --> 00:14:01,280
But I continued doing it.

224
00:14:01,280 --> 00:14:04,240
So instead of having a regular Boy Scout experience,

225
00:14:04,320 --> 00:14:07,120
that became more along the line of a Loan Scout.

226
00:14:08,640 --> 00:14:12,960
So Bill gets a copy of Scouting for Boys

227
00:14:13,600 --> 00:14:18,240
and he starts a patrol and the patrol works for just so long.

228
00:14:18,800 --> 00:14:23,680
You know they're 10 year old boys and they last for a few months.

229
00:14:23,680 --> 00:14:28,320
but then, as he said, we hadn't really caught the spirit of scouting yet.

230
00:14:28,320 --> 00:14:33,840
So the- whatever it was that caused the patrol to fly apart,

231
00:14:34,480 --> 00:14:35,600
you know that happened.

232
00:14:36,320 --> 00:14:40,720
And then the next thing that happens is really interesting.

233
00:14:40,720 --> 00:14:42,720
So let's listen to Bill talk about that.

234
00:14:43,280 --> 00:14:47,920
So it was quite a while until I finally, around 1915,, 16,,

235
00:14:48,880 --> 00:14:53,120
when I was about 15, I got myself established in a real patrol

236
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:57,440
and then eventually became the patrol that formed the basis

237
00:14:57,440 --> 00:14:58,880
for the patrol. it was handled

238
00:14:59,680 --> 00:15:03,600
because that was the patrol that really lived the patrol life,

239
00:15:03,680 --> 00:15:05,840
because we had a very good scout master.

240
00:15:05,840 --> 00:15:10,880
The troop was good too, and we had a lot of different experience.

241
00:15:10,880 --> 00:15:14,080
And that's where I learned about patrol leadership,

242
00:15:14,080 --> 00:15:16,160
because he would have us in his home-

243
00:15:16,160 --> 00:15:18,720
He was a bachelor and we would sit down

244
00:15:18,720 --> 00:15:20,080
and we were for patrol leader.

245
00:15:20,080 --> 00:15:25,360
We were discussing the troop program there,

246
00:15:26,560 --> 00:15:27,920
and then he would finally say to them:

247
00:15:27,920 --> 00:15:28,960
well, I'm going to bed now,

248
00:15:28,960 --> 00:15:31,520
You just continue until you get it finished,

249
00:15:32,079 --> 00:15:34,400
and we might head on for another half an hour.

250
00:15:35,520 --> 00:15:39,199
We're the one who really were running the troop before patrol leader,

251
00:15:39,840 --> 00:15:41,280
Which is the way it's supposed to work.

252
00:15:41,280 --> 00:15:41,600
Yeah,

253
00:15:42,480 --> 00:15:46,319
So now we know why adults get involved with this program:

254
00:15:46,880 --> 00:15:52,079
to help kind of even out some of the vicissitudes of youth.

255
00:15:52,079 --> 00:15:52,319
Right,

256
00:15:52,960 --> 00:15:55,840
Because they can get things going

257
00:15:55,840 --> 00:15:58,800
and they can work things out to a point.

258
00:15:59,520 --> 00:16:01,680
and then they need some experience

259
00:16:02,320 --> 00:16:05,040
that they don't yet have because of their years.

260
00:16:05,040 --> 00:16:06,880
They're not unintelligent,

261
00:16:06,880 --> 00:16:08,480
They're just inexperienced.

262
00:16:08,480 --> 00:16:11,040
Then they need someone with some experience

263
00:16:11,040 --> 00:16:13,440
to help them work things out.

264
00:16:13,440 --> 00:16:15,840
Now, at this point Bill's a little bit older

265
00:16:15,840 --> 00:16:18,080
and definitely has more capacity

266
00:16:18,080 --> 00:16:20,080
for working together with people

267
00:16:20,080 --> 00:16:22,320
in this type of relationship.

268
00:16:22,320 --> 00:16:23,760
What he was doing there

269
00:16:24,320 --> 00:16:27,280
is what really cemented the source code,

270
00:16:27,360 --> 00:16:29,439
if you will- in his mind.

271
00:16:29,439 --> 00:16:32,400
It got him to experientially understand

272
00:16:32,400 --> 00:16:34,240
what the possibilities were.

273
00:16:34,240 --> 00:16:35,760
So think about this for a minute.

274
00:16:35,760 --> 00:16:36,640
He's 10.

275
00:16:36,640 --> 00:16:39,600
He gets a copy of the Scout Handbook for Christmas,

276
00:16:39,600 --> 00:16:41,520
Goes out and forms his own patrol

277
00:16:41,520 --> 00:16:43,680
and is thinking about all the wonderful things

278
00:16:43,680 --> 00:16:44,560
that he gets to do.

279
00:16:44,560 --> 00:16:48,240
He gets to go camping and gets to go out in the woods

280
00:16:48,240 --> 00:16:51,360
and tracking and all of these great scout skills.

281
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:55,760
But the piece that is missing to his 10-year-old mind

282
00:16:55,760 --> 00:16:59,520
is the broader spectrum of what scouting is

283
00:16:59,520 --> 00:17:01,920
and that is the development of character

284
00:17:01,920 --> 00:17:04,079
through working together with other people.

285
00:17:04,079 --> 00:17:07,280
And it takes some years for him to realize this

286
00:17:07,280 --> 00:17:10,079
and to get himself into a position,

287
00:17:10,640 --> 00:17:12,480
with some adult guidance,

288
00:17:12,480 --> 00:17:16,400
where he is actually going to start making that happen

289
00:17:16,400 --> 00:17:20,560
And that whole world of that part of Scouts

290
00:17:20,560 --> 00:17:21,280
opens up to him.

291
00:17:21,280 --> 00:17:24,079
So it's no longer just the skills

292
00:17:24,079 --> 00:17:26,560
and the activities that are attracting him

293
00:17:26,560 --> 00:17:29,919
but the broader ulterior motives of scouting.

294
00:17:29,919 --> 00:17:33,520
That's kind of my take on Bill's story thus far.

295
00:17:33,520 --> 00:17:35,280
So what happens to Bill next

296
00:17:35,280 --> 00:17:37,840
is another interesting part of the story.

297
00:17:37,840 --> 00:17:38,960
Let's hear that.

298
00:17:39,840 --> 00:17:43,040
And then it happened that I reached just the white leadership

299
00:17:43,040 --> 00:17:46,560
and the white advancement. in 1920,

300
00:17:47,280 --> 00:17:49,120
when Ben Powell called the Scout of the World

301
00:17:49,120 --> 00:17:51,040
together for the first World Jumper Reef,

302
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:55,280
And in Denmark, where the Scout movement had been established

303
00:17:55,280 --> 00:17:57,120
by them for about 10 years,

304
00:17:58,080 --> 00:18:01,600
he was decided that every troop should send a representative

305
00:18:01,600 --> 00:18:03,280
to that first Jumper Reef.

306
00:18:03,280 --> 00:18:05,920
And I was a lucky one who was picked to go there

307
00:18:06,879 --> 00:18:09,520
And that's where I met Ben Powell for the first time,

308
00:18:09,520 --> 00:18:11,120
and on his birthday-

309
00:18:11,120 --> 00:18:14,080
on my birthday, the 6th of August of 1920,-

310
00:18:14,080 --> 00:18:16,159
we proclaimed him Chief Scout of the World

311
00:18:17,600 --> 00:18:19,120
And then, because the Danish team

312
00:18:19,120 --> 00:18:22,080
had won the World Championship to that first Jumper Reef,

313
00:18:22,720 --> 00:18:25,440
Denmark had the first choice of having the second Jumper Reef

314
00:18:25,440 --> 00:18:26,480
come to Denmark.

315
00:18:26,480 --> 00:18:28,160
And then it was 1924.

316
00:18:28,960 --> 00:18:31,440
So by then I had seen the Scouts of the World

317
00:18:31,440 --> 00:18:32,880
and two different Jumper Reefs

318
00:18:34,080 --> 00:18:36,720
And that had become a master science in pharmacy.

319
00:18:36,720 --> 00:18:39,040
but I just dropped out of the whole pharmacy

320
00:18:39,040 --> 00:18:44,400
and became a newspaper journalist for the 1924 Jumper Reef.

321
00:18:45,360 --> 00:18:46,800
So here he goes,

322
00:18:46,800 --> 00:18:49,680
He's off to the first World Jumper Reef.

323
00:18:49,680 --> 00:18:53,040
As he said, because the Danish Scouts took the top honors

324
00:18:53,040 --> 00:18:55,040
at the first World Jumper Reef,

325
00:18:55,040 --> 00:18:57,760
they got to host the second World Jumper Reef.

326
00:18:57,760 --> 00:19:00,400
Now what may not be clear from the recording

327
00:19:00,400 --> 00:19:03,920
is: Bill was a student studying pharmacology

328
00:19:04,480 --> 00:19:07,440
and decided that he would be a journalist

329
00:19:07,440 --> 00:19:09,760
for that second World Jumper Reef.

330
00:19:09,760 --> 00:19:12,000
So after the second World Jumper Reef,

331
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:16,240
Bill decides in his vast wisdom as a 25-year-old

332
00:19:16,240 --> 00:19:18,400
that he's going to do something else,

333
00:19:18,400 --> 00:19:20,320
And let's listen to that part of the story.

334
00:19:21,920 --> 00:19:24,480
And then the next year I felt myself getting rather old,

335
00:19:24,480 --> 00:19:26,800
I was getting in a vicinity of 25..

336
00:19:27,600 --> 00:19:29,440
And I decided how it could see the world

337
00:19:29,440 --> 00:19:30,720
and meet some of these fellows

338
00:19:30,720 --> 00:19:33,520
that I had to get down where it had to get started.

339
00:19:34,560 --> 00:19:37,040
So I decided to take off on a trip around the world,

340
00:19:37,040 --> 00:19:38,800
which was an absolute lunia idea.

341
00:19:39,360 --> 00:19:41,440
I came to the United States

342
00:19:41,440 --> 00:19:44,159
after having spent some time in Europe

343
00:19:44,159 --> 00:19:47,120
and after having sat down in Liverpool, England,

344
00:19:47,120 --> 00:19:50,480
to write a book that paid for my trip to the United States.

345
00:19:51,840 --> 00:19:56,000
And then, as soon as I got to the United States,

346
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,399
I got in touch with a person who had been

347
00:19:58,399 --> 00:20:01,360
the leader of the American contingent

348
00:20:01,360 --> 00:20:02,879
for the Danish Jumper Reef

349
00:20:02,879 --> 00:20:06,000
And he got me established in the New York Boy Scouts Camp

350
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:07,200
for the whole summer.

351
00:20:07,200 --> 00:20:11,200
So that's how I got to learn about Boy Scouting in America,

352
00:20:11,200 --> 00:20:13,360
the best part of it and the worst part of it.

353
00:20:14,400 --> 00:20:17,200
So he starts this big trip.

354
00:20:17,200 --> 00:20:19,200
He's going to see the world.

355
00:20:19,200 --> 00:20:20,800
He's going to see the world of Scouting,

356
00:20:20,800 --> 00:20:23,600
spends time in Europe, spends time in England

357
00:20:23,600 --> 00:20:25,600
and ends up in the United States.

358
00:20:25,600 --> 00:20:28,000
And that's where I'm going to stop for this week.

359
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,800
We'll continue the story next time around,

360
00:20:30,800 --> 00:20:34,400
But here's what I think are the important takeaways:

361
00:20:34,800 --> 00:20:40,800
Greenbar Bill- obviously one of the most influential voices

362
00:20:40,800 --> 00:20:42,400
in the Scouting movement

363
00:20:42,400 --> 00:20:44,800
and in the Boy Scouts of America.

364
00:20:44,800 --> 00:20:51,200
We don't have that one influential voice right now

365
00:20:51,200 --> 00:20:55,200
And I don't think that we need that personality

366
00:20:55,200 --> 00:20:58,800
because we have the source code, don't we?

367
00:20:58,800 --> 00:21:02,400
We have the ideas that make this work

368
00:21:02,400 --> 00:21:04,400
And when we put them to work,

369
00:21:04,400 --> 00:21:06,400
no matter what organization we're a part of,

370
00:21:06,400 --> 00:21:08,400
it's going to work.

371
00:21:08,400 --> 00:21:10,400
We're going to have Scouting

372
00:21:10,400 --> 00:21:14,400
And we're going to positively affect the lives of the children

373
00:21:14,400 --> 00:21:18,400
in our communities with the program that's called Scouting

374
00:21:18,400 --> 00:21:20,400
when we apply the source code.

375
00:21:20,400 --> 00:21:22,400
That's where I want to leave us this week.

376
00:21:22,400 --> 00:21:24,400
We'll continue this discussion

